## How This Relates to Inflation

**The inflation picture:**
- **CPI (May 2026, most recent):** 333.98 (index, base 1982-84=100)
- **One year ago (July 2025):** 322.17
- **Year-over-year increase:** +3.6% annualized rate

The CPI index has risen steadily over the past year, with acceleration visible in the **March-April 2026 period** — precisely when crude prices spiked. This is **not coincidental**:

| Period | WTI $/bbl | CPI Index | Interpretation |
|--------|-----------|-----------|---|
| July 2025 | $67.48 | 322.17 | Baseline |
| Feb 2026 | $63.99 | 327.46 | Modest gain |
| **Mar 2026** | **$78–105** | **330.29** | Oil spike begins hitting CPI |
| **Apr 2026** | **$93–105** | **332.41** | Momentum continues |
| May 2026 | $102–105 | 333.98 | Elevated plateau |

**The transmission mechanism:**
- Energy prices (crude → gasoline, diesel, heating) are **direct components of CPI**
- Energy inflation ripples into **transportation costs, which lift core services inflation** (freight, logistics)
- **Broader wage-price dynamics:** If energy inflation is sustained, workers demand higher wages, which can feed into persistent services inflation

**Current macro context:**
- Fed funds rate: **3.63%** (as of May 2026)
- Unemployment: **4.30%** (May 2026)
- 10-year Treasury yield: **4.49%** (June 17, 2026)

The Fed is maintaining a moderately restrictive stance (real rates ~1% above the fed funds rate if CPI is running ~3.6%). This reflects an economy still showing inflation pressure despite elevated rates.

---

## Bottom Line

Gas prices (driven by crude oil near $85/bbl, up 27% YoY) are currently a **material upward driver of headline CPI inflation**. The sharp March crude spike syncs with visible CPI acceleration in March–April 2026. Whether this feeds into **persistent inflation** or remains a temporary energy shock depends on whether crude stabilizes here and whether wage-price dynamics remain contained — two factors the Fed is actively monitoring.
